{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794437850,"nonce":"dIWXNydoa1DK6ApG9I8FAr1KCU4=","title":"Animated cards with text cost about 16 MB each on an iPhone, and 200 of them get the app killed","message":"Found by the GPU-deferral baseline (07f1e4b). iPhone 12 Pro Max, mobile/example release build, scene `cards`: a grid of small cards, each under a looping CSS keyframe animation of transform and opacity, each holding one short text.\n\nMemory by count, steady: 20 cards 490 to 700 MB, 60 cards 1.1 to 1.6 GB, 100 cards 1.9 to 2.1 GB, 140 cards 2.5 GB. At 200 the app is killed about 2 s after launch, every run. That is about 16 to 18 MB per card, close to one full-screen RGBA texture (1284x2778x4 = 14.3 MB). The cause is not confirmed. The same 200 cards without text hold 885 MB and run at 36 fps, so the text is what changes the cost per card.\n\nFrame cost grows with the count too: render 6.9 ms at 20 cards, 17.5 at 60, 31 at 100, 49 at 140, about 0.35 ms per card.\n\nOn the desktop the same scene takes the full paint path every frame at about 48 ms on an M1 Pro, against 8 ms for the cards without text, which take the fast path. A Metal trace of the no-text scene on the phone shows about 12,100 `Tight Blit Pass` and 12,300 `PendingWrites` encoders in 3 s, roughly one blit and one upload per animated card per frame.\n\nTo do: find what allocates per card (a layer texture per animated element with a text descendant is the suspect), and put a ceiling on it. Method and tables are in the baseline comment on 07f1e4b; the scene is `BLINC_BENCH=cards`, with `BLINC_BENCH_CARDS=N` for the count.","files":null}]}